{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>I will see what their end shall be, for they are a generation of reversals.</b> Israel are called by three names: יַעֲקֹב, יִשְׂרָאֵל, יְשֻׁרוּן, and the final letters of these names spell לָבָן ('white'), on account of the merit of Jacob, that HaShem whitens their transgressions. But they reversed the word לָבָן into נָבָל ('base'), as it is written above: ״עַם נָבָל וְלֹא חָכָם״ — as our Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Sanhedrin 97a): the generation of Mashiach will turn everything to heresy. What is the verse? As it is said (Leviticus 13:13): ״כֻּלּוֹ הָפַךְ לָבָן טָהוֹר הוּא״ — which means that the reversal of the word לָבָן is נָבָל. Therefore it says here: ״אֶרְאֶה מָה אַחֲרִיתָם״ — for the end (אַחֲרִית) of these three names yields the letters לָבָן, alluding to that He would whiten their deeds, but they are a generation of reversals (תַּהְפֻּכֹת) who reversed לָבָן into נָבָל, as it is written: ״עַם נָבָל וְלֹא חָכָם.״ And above I explained the verse ״עַם נָבָל״ as referring to one who loves silver and is a miser, and concerning him it is said (Jeremiah 17:11): ״עֹשֶׂה עֹשֶׁר וְלֹא בְמִשְׁפָּט וְגוֹ׳ וּבְאַחֲרִיתוֹ יִהְיֶה נָבָל״ — as it is written here: ״אֶרְאֶה מָה אַחֲרִיתָם.״" "And some support for this: the matter begins with נָבָל and ends with נָבָל. It begins with it, as it is said: ״וַיְנַבֵּל צוּר יְשׁוּעָתוֹ״, and it ends with it, as it is said: ״בְּגוֹי נָבָל אַכְעִיסֵם.״ This is what it says: ״כִּי דוֹר תַּהְפֻּכוֹת הֵמָּה״ — they reverse לָבָן into נָבָל, and from this side they are ״בָּנִים לֹא אֵמֻן בָּם.״ ״הֵם קִנְאוּנִי בְּלֹא אֵל״ — for they also reversed the word אֵל, which reversed is לֹא, for they denied HaShem and said לֹא הוּא (ibid. 5:12). ״כִּעֲסוּנִי בְּהַבְלֵיהֶם״ — to reverse all the words. And corresponding to these two reversals, I ״אַקְנִיאֵם בְּלֹא עָם״, to say to them ״לֹא עַמִּי אַתֶּם״ (Hosea 2:1): with לֹא they sinned and with לֹא they will be stricken; with נָבָל they sinned and with נָבָל they will be stricken — this is what it says: ״בְּגוֹי נָבָל אַכְעִיסֵם.״ Therefore it says: ״לוּ חָכְמוּ יָבִינוּ לְאַחֲרִיתָם״ — which is לָבָן, that they would whiten their deeds through repentance to return to HaShem, concerning whom it is said (Daniel 7:9): ״לְבוּשֵׁיהּ כִּתְלַג חִוָּר וּשְׂעַר רֵישֵׁיהּ כַּעֲמַר נְקֵא.״ And then He would fulfill in them: ״אִם יִהְיוּ חֲטָאֵיכֶם כַּשָּׁנִים כַּשֶּׁלֶג יַלְבִּינוּ וְאִם יַאְדִּימוּ כַתּוֹלָע כַּצֶּמֶר יִהְיוּ״ (Isaiah 1:18)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The wordplay throughout depends on the Hebrew letter reversal: לבן (white) read backwards becomes נבל (base/fool); similarly אל (El) reversed becomes לא (no/not). The translation preserves both Hebrew forms in each pair."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:14.633659248Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}